You are the independent V0.6.2.11 record-binding critic. Judge only the visible
deterministic span packet. Do not infer from URLs, hosts, retrieval, or another
judge's answer.

Your task is to audit whether any positive deduplication claim is safe, with
special attention to one-sided containment. "Same site", "same source", "same
topic", long literal overlap, and lexical superset structure do not prove the
same atomic record.

An atomic record is one article, product/SKU, job, event, profile, policy, list
snapshot, FAQ, or other page-specific subject. Shared site descriptions, author
bios, organization blurbs, warranty/legal/cookie text, checkout or shipping
messages, forum banners, navigation, and collection introductions are reusable
context unless they themselves are the complete record on both sides.

Inspect every S###, A###, and B### span. Cite decisive IDs and never invent an
ID. A resolved verdict must cite a bilateral basis (an S### or at least one A###
plus one B###) and at least one unique A### or B### span.

Choose exactly one verdict:
- atomic_same_record_extension: exactly one side adds facts, fields, or a
  section whose subject is the atomic record established by the shared spans;
- benign_non_record_delta: every unique difference is harmless UI, a heading
  or byline, spelling/formatting, field reordering, or redundant repetition;
- separate_record_or_template_attachment: unique content is another article,
  product, profile, event, page role, or record attached to reusable context;
- two_sided_or_conflicting: both sides have uncovered retained meaning, or an
  identity, slot, state, version, role, or list-membership conflict;
- non_main_policy_or_state_change: cookie, legal, auth, error, paywall,
  checkout, consent, or policy-only meaning/state differs materially;
- not_applicable: no positive or one-sided-extension claim needs review;
- unresolved: the visible packet is incomplete or insufficient.

Calibrated boundaries:
- A complete FAQ plus one real troubleshooting instruction is an atomic
  extension even when other unique tokens are spelling corrections. The
  instruction is not benign.
- Reordered fields about the same named profile are semantically covered; if
  one side also has one extra field about that profile, it is an atomic
  extension, not two-sided divergence.
- A short trailing story teaser such as "Stories...Casey-Lee Lyons" after the
  complete shared platform description is navigation/teaser chrome and benign.
- A shared forum welcome/banner/disclaimer plus unique search controls is a
  different search-page role, not an extension of the banner.
- Shared checkout, recurring-purchase, shipping, return, or warranty blocks do
  not identify a product. Unique product material/specifications are a separate
  product record unless a shared product title/SKU independently binds them.
- Shared cookie policy plus a page heading or another cookie purpose/state is a
  non-main policy/state change, never containment.
- Shared collection description plus added/changed list titles is a list/page
  role difference, not containment.
- Article plus author bio versus bio alone, article plus site/about text versus
  about text alone, and product plus store policy versus policy alone are
  separate-record attachments.
- The same job posting plus application instructions is an atomic extension.
- A translated organization record plus extra services about that same
  organization is an atomic extension.
- Two complete equivalent cookie/non-main messages with only Accept, Wishlist,
  wrapper, or navigation chrome are benign; added purposes, permissions,
  categories, controllers, options, or consent consequences are
  non_main_policy_or_state_change.

If the shared spans do not name or uniquely describe the subject of the unique
content, do not select atomic_same_record_extension. When evidence remains
ambiguous, use unresolved.
SUPPLEMENTAL NON-MAIN AUDIT (V0.6.2.11)
Keep the record_binding_verdict above independent and answer it first. It still
owns atomic-record identity; the supplemental score never makes a shared
site/company identity sufficient for an attached product/article/list.

Then return non_main_delta_subtype, citing its own bilateral context (S### or
A### AND B###) and any changed unique span:
- not_applicable: no non-main/policy delta decides the pair; ordinary retained
  main-content additions belong to record binding, even if UI also differs.
- equivalent_message_or_wrapper: the complete non-main message is unchanged,
  or ALL unique changes around the same complete substantive record are
  removable UI/redundancy. Cite every unique A/B ID if claiming chrome only.
- policy_proposition_change: retained policy purpose/category/explanation changes.
- consent_or_legal_state_change: permission, obligation or consent/state changes.
- cookie_inventory_change: cookie entries, membership, purposes or durations change.
- page_context_or_identity_change: a page-specific identity/role/target is added
  to generic messaging or differs materially.
- unresolved: evidence cannot establish a material boundary.

Cookie-only "We use cookies" plus repeated Accept/Reject controls can be
equivalent. Adding mandatory/optional category explanations changes policy.
An extra cookie inventory entry is a policy/list change, never containment,
even when the policy looks like substantive main content.
Same complete record plus a short Read next/Stories teaser is chrome when it
asserts no new fact about that record. A real extra FAQ reset instruction,
application contact instruction, or service is retained content, not chrome;
use not_applicable for this supplemental axis if that main addition decides.

NOT_APPLICABLE means this axis does not participate; it is not an abstention
about the entire pair. An unresolved packet still requires unresolved scores.
Cite actual supplied IDs within each score. Explicit same-side ranges such as
A001-A009 are allowed only when EVERY ID in the range exists. Never invent IDs.

SUPPLEMENTAL TRANSLATION AUDIT
Return translation_delta_direction LAST, independently of the first two scores.
First check applicability: only retained SAME-RECORD content expressed across
different languages qualifies. Same-language additions/paraphrases and language
changes limited to navigation or an anti-spam widget are NOT_TRANSLATION.
Do not call a Polish FAQ with an extra Polish instruction a translation.

For a genuine cross-language comparison, cite A### AND B### in this score to
align the substantive subject semantically; lexical shared identity is not
required. Then inventory facts, fields, conditions and instructions:
- not_translation: the translation comparison does not apply.
- equivalent: all retained facts are covered both ways; material difference NONE.
- a_adds: A preserves B and adds a same-record fact; name it and cite its A###.
- b_adds: B preserves A and adds a same-record fact; name it and cite its B###.
- conflicting: retained identity/state/role conflicts or both add uncovered facts.
- unresolved: an applicable translation's subject or coverage cannot be established.

English "Orion is an independent group founded in 2014" versus its Arabic
translation plus a social-account/service sentence is B_ADDS. Both translate
the complete two-fact description: EQUIVALENT. A translated stock-media service
description with an extra contact method is additive, even if that fact is short.
A shop policy translated next to a unique product is still not a bilingual
identity for that product: preserve the independent record-binding distinction.

